      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the length of the tunnel given the speed of the train and the time it takes for the tail of the train to exit the tunnel after the front has entered.\n\n1. **Convert the train's speed to miles per minute**:\n   The train's speed is 60 miles per hour. Since there are 60 minutes in an hour, we can convert the speed to miles per minute:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Speed of the train} = \\frac{60 \\text{ miles}}{60 \\text{ minutes}} = 1 \\text{ mile per minute}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the distance traveled in 3 minutes**:\n   Since the train is traveling at a speed of 1 mile per minute, in 3 minutes, the train will have traveled:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance traveled} = 1 \\text{ mile per minute} \\times 3 \\text{ minutes} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Relate the distance traveled to the length of the train**:\n   The distance traveled (3 miles) is the sum of the length of the train (1 mile) and the length of the tunnel (let's call the length of the tunnel \\( L \\)):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance traveled} = \\text{Length of the train} + \\text{Length of the tunnel}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known values:\n   \\[\n   3 \\text{ miles} = 1 \\text{ mile} + L\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for the length of the tunnel**:\n   To find \\( L \\), we subtract the length of the train from the total distance traveled:\n   \\[\n   L = 3 \\text{ miles} - 1 \\text{ mile} = 2 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the tunnel is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) miles.",
