      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given:\n- The train is 1 mile long.\n- The train enters the tunnel in 3 minutes.\n- The train is moving at 60 miles per hour.\n\nFirst, we convert the speed of the train from miles per hour to miles per minute. Since there are 60 minutes in an hour, we have:\n\\[\n60 \\text{ miles per hour} = \\frac{60 \\text{ miles}}{60 \\text{ minutes}} = 1 \\text{ mile per minute}\n\\]\n\nNext, we determine the distance the train travels in 3 minutes. Since the train moves at 1 mile per minute, in 3 minutes it will travel:\n\\[\n1 \\text{ mile per minute} \\times 3 \\text{ minutes} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nThis distance of 3 miles is the total distance the train travels from the moment the front of the train enters the tunnel until the moment the tail of the train exits the tunnel. This 3-mile distance encompasses the length of the train itself plus the length of the tunnel.\n\nSince the length of the train is 1 mile, we can find the length of the tunnel by subtracting the length of the train from the total distance traveled by the train:\n\\[\n\\text{Length of the tunnel} = \\text{Total distance traveled} - \\text{Length of the train} = 3 \\text{ miles} - 1 \\text{ mile} = 2 \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the tunnel is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
