      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Determine the time it takes for the train to travel the length of the tunnel plus the length of the train (the whole train is inside the tunnel):**\n\n   The front of the train enters the tunnel at time \\( t = 0 \\) minutes, and the end of the train exits the tunnel exactly 3 minutes later. The length of the train is 1 mile, so the total distance the train travels to completely exit the tunnel is \\( 1 + 1 = 2 \\) miles.\n\n   Using the speed of the train, which is 60 miles per hour, we convert this speed into miles per minute:\n   \\[\n   60 \\text{ miles per hour} = \\frac{60}{60} = 1 \\text{ mile per minute}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the time \\( t \\) it takes for the train to travel 2 miles is:\n   \\[\n   t = \\frac{2 \\text{ miles}}{1 \\text{ mile per minute}} = 2 \\text{ minutes}\n   \\]\n   Since this time is given as 3 minutes, the actual time taken is:\n   \\[\n   t = 3 \\text{ minutes}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the distance the front of the train travels in 3 minutes:**\n\n   Using the speed of the train again:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = 1 \\text{ mile per minute} \\times 3 \\text{ minutes} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the length of the tunnel:**\n\n   The distance the front of the train travels to exit the tunnel is the length of the tunnel plus the length of the train. Therefore, the length of the tunnel \\( L \\) is:\n   \\[\n   L + 1 = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( L \\):\n   \\[\n   L = 3 - 1 = 2 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the length of the tunnel is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) miles.",
