      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the length of the tunnel given the conditions of the train's movement. Let's break it down step by step.\n\n1. **Convert the train's speed to miles per minute:**\n   The train is moving at a speed of 60 miles per hour. First, we convert this speed into miles per minute.\n   \\[\n   \\frac{60 \\text{ miles}}{60 \\text{ minutes}} = 1 \\text{ mile per minute}\n   \\]\n   So, the train travels 1 mile in one minute.\n\n2. **Determine the time taken for the train to completely exit the tunnel:**\n   The train exits the tunnel exactly 3 minutes after the front of the train enters the tunnel. This means the train travels 1 mile in 3 minutes.\n\n3. **Calculate the length of the train:**\n   The length of the train is given as 1 mile.\n\n4. **Determine the total distance the front of the train travels in 3 minutes:**\n   Since the train travels 1 mile in 3 minutes, the distance the front of the train travels in 3 minutes is:\n   \\[\n   1 \\text{ mile}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Identify the total distance traveled in 3 minutes:**\n   The total distance traveled by the front of the train in 3 minutes is the length of the tunnel plus the length of the train, because the front of the train exits the tunnel when it has traveled the total length of the tunnel plus the length of the train.\n   Let \\( L \\) be the length of the tunnel. The total distance traveled is:\n   \\[\n   L + 1 \\text{ mile}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Set up the equation:**\n   The front of the train travels 1 mile in 3 minutes, so in 3 minutes, the front of the train should have traveled the length of the tunnel plus the length of the train. Therefore, we can write:\n   \\[\n   L + 1 = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Solve for the length of the tunnel:**\n   Subtract 1 mile from both sides of the equation:\n   \\[\n   L = 3 - 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   L = 2 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the length of the tunnel is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
